              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Resistance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 magnetic levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dealers and manual agree</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Connectivity</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ANT+ and Bluetooth; Kinomap, Zwift, Aviron</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dealers and manual agree</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 yr frame, 2 yr parts, 1 yr labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dealers give 5 yr frame, 2 yr parts; manual gives a generic 1-year term</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to read the conflicts</h3>
          <p>
            The pattern suggests a machine sourced from a contract manufacturer, with a generic owner's
            manual written for the factory's own version and a sell sheet written by TKO for the US
            market. That is common in commercial fitness equipment and is not in itself a warning sign.
            But it means the manual's figures and the sell sheet's cannot both be right for the unit you
            receive, and the difference matters most for capacity. A sell sheet saying 350 lb and a manual
            saying 150 kg (about 330 lb) is a 20 lb gap at the top of the range; if you or your members are
            near it, ask TKO which figure applies and get the answer in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The machine weight conflict matters mainly for delivery. Iron Company lists a boxed shipping
            weight of 414 lb and a box of 88 by 40 by 34 inches, so whichever net figure is right, this is a
            pallet delivery that needs several people or equipment to move indoors. The running surface
            width — 17.3 inches, or 440 mm — is the one dimension every source agrees on. That is a
            typical curved-treadmill width, a little wider than the AssaultRunner Pro's 17 inches and
            narrower than the 19 to 20 inches on some SB Fitness and SOLE machines. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains how much margin to leave between your weight and a rating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-trains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How a Curved Treadmill With Magnetic Resistance Trains</h2>
<p>
            The 8CTM has no motor. You drive a belt of slats around a concave curve: step forward on the
            curve and the belt speeds up, drift back and it slows. There is no top speed and no speed
            setting, and the belt stops when you do. TKO's manual describes the four resistance levels
            this way: level 1 is free running without resistance, level 2 adds slight resistance to
            simulate weighted jogging, and levels 3 and 4 add enough to simulate pushing a sled. TKO's sell
            sheet adds that the handrails are placed for casual walking or for push-sled work.
          </p>
          <p>
            That range is the 8CTM's main reason to exist. A free-running curve suits maximal sprints;
            heavy resistance turns the same machine into a sled-push station for leg drive and power; and
            the middle settings steady the belt for controlled running. For a CrossFit box or a team
            weight room, one machine that covers sprints and sled work in a few square feet is a genuine
            space saving. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-sprint-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">sprint workout guide</a>
            and
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a>
            show the kinds of sessions that use it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The format's limits still apply</h3>
          <p>
            Resistance does not change the basic character of a curved treadmill. It is harder work than
            a motorised belt at the same pace, because you are moving the belt yourself. Holding one steady
            pace takes attention, since you hold it by your position on the curve. And slow walking is
            awkward on any curve, which is why our
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>
            warns against buying one mainly to walk. If your training is mostly easy aerobic miles at a set
            pace, a motorised treadmill is the better tool; our
            <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorized comparison</a>
            sets out the trade-off, and our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-manual-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a manual treadmill</a>
            covers the first sessions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Console, Zwift and Kinomap</h2>
<p>
            TKO lists a console that reads time, speed, distance, calories, watts and RPM, with heart-rate
            control (HRC), target heart rate (THR), 10/20 and 20/10 interval programmes and a custom
            interval option. The manual adds pace, a MET reading and a resistance readout, and an ANT+
            button for pairing a heart-rate strap.
          </p>
          <p>
            Connectivity is the 8CTM's clearest advantage over simpler curved machines at lower prices.
            TKO's manual describes Bluetooth pairing as a treadmill using the FTMS standard, with
            step-by-step instructions for Kinomap and for Zwift's "run speed" device. That means the
            belt's speed can drive your avatar in Zwift without a foot pod, which most battery-console
            curved treadmills cannot do. The manual also notes that the console wakes when the belt moves
            and goes to sleep, turning Bluetooth off, after about four and a half minutes without
            movement, so expect to re-pair after a long break. Our
            <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            explains how treadmill pairing works, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for Zwift</a>
            roundup compares other options.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treat speed and calorie readings on any self-powered treadmill as estimates. Speed comes from
            a sensor reading the belt, and calories from a formula; our guides to
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed accuracy</a>
            and
            <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">calorie accuracy</a>
            explain the margins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Warranty, Returns and Delivery</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The warranty</h3>
          <p>
            TKO's May 2026 sell sheet gives 5 years on the frame, 2 years on parts and 1 year on labour,
            excluding normal wear and tear. Select Fitness and Iron Company list the same frame and parts
            terms, and The Fitness Outlet adds the 1-year labour term. The owner's manual, by contrast,
            contains a generic "implied warranty" of one year and states that the device is intended for
            private use and that the warranty does not apply to professional use — wording that sits
            oddly with TKO marketing the machine to health clubs and schools. We read that as factory
            boilerplate rather than TKO's US terms, but ask TKO or your dealer to confirm in writing which
            terms apply, and whether they differ for home and commercial use.
          </p>
          <p>
            For comparison, TKO's newer 8CRUN lists 10 years on the frame and 3 on parts, the SB Fitness
            CT700 lists 10 years on the frame for home use, and Assault lists 5 or 7 years on the
            AssaultRunner Pro's frame depending on the source. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            lists the questions to ask.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns</h3>
          <p>
            TKO's own policy allows returns for a refund up to 30 days from receipt, with a return
            authorisation number obtained first. Change-of-mind returns carry a 15% restocking fee, and
            special-order items 25%. Items must be unused, in original condition and in their original
            packaging, and the buyer pays return shipping, which is not refunded. TKO replaces items only
            if they are defective or damaged. Dealer policies differ — Select Fitness, for example, gives
            30 days with a refund minus return shipping on unused items, and an exchange or store credit
            on used ones — so read the policy of the seller you buy from. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">return policies guide</a>
            compares the common terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery and assembly</h3>
          <p>
            Expect freight delivery on a pallet to the kerb or loading dock. Iron Company describes
            kerbside delivery with liftgate service only, next-business-day delivery after 24 hours'
            notice, inside delivery at extra cost, and someone present to inspect and sign. Inspect the
            crate before signing and note any damage on the delivery receipt. TKO's manual covers
            assembly of the console, uprights and handrails, which includes connecting communication and
            brake cables; plan on two people, or pay a dealer to install it. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">delivery guide</a>
            and
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembly guide</a>
            cover what to arrange.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Safety and recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for TKO and the AirRaid
            range on 14 October 2026 and found no recall or warning. Hold the rails until you can control
            the belt, start sled-push sessions at the lower resistance levels, and keep children and pets
            away from the moving slats.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Who It Suits, and What to Consider Instead</h2>
<p>
            <strong>It suits gyms, boxes and team facilities.</strong> Four magnetic levels covering
            sprints and sled pushes, a commercial-grade build, a 350 lb rating on TKO's sheet, and a brand
            that sells through commercial dealers make the 8CTM a natural fit for a facility that wants
            one curved machine to do several jobs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It suits Zwift and Kinomap users who want a curved belt.</strong> Bluetooth FTMS
            pairing is unusual on a self-powered curved treadmill, and it removes the need for a foot pod.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Home buyers should compare prices first.</strong> At $5,000 to $5,500, the 8CTM costs
            roughly twice as much as some curved treadmills with more resistance levels and longer home
            warranties. If you do not need the connectivity or the commercial build, you are paying for
            things you will not use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walkers should look elsewhere.</strong> A curved, self-powered belt is a poor tool for
            slow walking. Our
            <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual treadmill roundup</a>
            and walking guides cover better options.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Alternatives on this site</h3>
          <p>
            The <strong>AssaultRunner Pro</strong> lists a 17 by 62-inch surface, a 350 lb rating, a 280 lb
            frame and a battery console with Bluetooth and ANT+ at about $3,499. It has no resistance
            setting, but it is the most widely reviewed curved trainer for home use; see our
            <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AssaultRunner Pro review</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <strong>SB Fitness CT700</strong> lists eight magnetic resistance levels, stainless steel
            bearings and a 19 by 66.6-inch deck at $2,595 direct, with a ten-year residential frame warranty.
            Its figures come mainly from the seller and conflict in places, but on paper it does much of
            what the 8CTM does for half the price, without app connectivity; see our
            <a href="/sb-fitness-ct700-curved-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SB Fitness CT700 review</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <strong>Pro 6 Arcadia Air Runner</strong> is the closest match on price, at $4,495 to
            $4,595, with six resistance levels and a watt-reading console, but it publishes no user weight
            limit and a one-year parts warranty; see our
            <a href="/pro-6-arcadia-air-runner/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Arcadia Air Runner review</a>.
            And the <strong>SOLE C80</strong> in our
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a>
            lists eight magnetic levels, a 62 by 19-inch belt and a 330 lb rating at around $2,200 to $2,400.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            The TKO AirRaid Runner manual treadmill 8CTM — now the AirRaid Force on TKO's own site — is a
            capable commercial curved treadmill. Four magnetic resistance levels, a 350 lb rating on TKO's
            sell sheet, Bluetooth FTMS for Zwift and Kinomap and a 5-year frame warranty make it a strong
            fit for facilities and serious athletes who want sprint and sled work on one self-powered
            machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Its weaknesses are price and paperwork. It costs $5,000 to $5,500, TKO's own documents
            disagree on several figures, and the AirRaid Runner name now belongs to a different model. Buy
            it from a dealer who will put the model number, capacity and warranty in writing. For home use,
            compare it first with the AssaultRunner Pro and the SB Fitness CT700, which cost far less. We
            have not tested the 8CTM; this verdict rests on TKO's documents, dealer listings and the terms
            described above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the TKO AirRaid Runner 8CTM the same as the TKO AirRaid Force?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in practice. TKO’s own site lists the 8CTM-B as the TKO AirRaid Force, and its May 2026 sell sheet calls it the AirRaid Force Runner, while many dealers still sell the 8CTM as the AirRaid Runner. The same footprint, 350 lb rating and four-level magnetic resistance appear under both names.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the TKO 8CTM and the 8CRUN?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The 8CRUN is TKO’s newer AirRaid Runner. Its sell sheet lists a 61 x 17 in. surface, a 400 lb rating, a 290 lb net weight, a battery console and a 10-year frame warranty, with no resistance system listed, at $5,850 on request. The 8CTM has four magnetic resistance levels, a 350 lb rating and a 5-year frame warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit of the TKO AirRaid Runner 8CTM?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                TKO’s sell sheet and four US dealers list 350 lb. TKO’s generic owner’s manual gives 150 kg, about 330 lb. If you are near either figure, ask TKO which applies to your unit and get it in writing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the TKO AirRaid Runner work with Zwift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                TKO lists ANT+ and Bluetooth and names Kinomap, Zwift and Aviron, and its manual gives pairing steps for Kinomap and for Zwift’s run speed device using the Bluetooth FTMS standard. The console goes to sleep after about four and a half minutes without belt movement, which turns Bluetooth off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does the TKO AirRaid Runner 8CTM cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 14 October 2026 TKO listed it as the AirRaid Force at $5,500. Select Fitness and The Fitness Outlet listed it at $5,000 against $5,500, and Push Pedal Pull at $5,500. Freight is usually extra or quoted at checkout.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the TKO AirRaid Runner need electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The belt is self-powered: it moves only when you push it, and speed follows your position on the curve. TKO’s documents for the 8CTM do not state how the console is powered, so ask your dealer whether it uses batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a TKO AirRaid Runner</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">With two TKO curved treadmills sharing a name, most mistakes here are about which machine you are buying and on what terms. These are the four to avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering the wrong AirRaid model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Dealers sell the 8CTM as the AirRaid Runner; TKO now uses that name for the 8CRUN, which has no resistance system and a different warranty. Put the model number on your order and check it against the specification on the invoice before you sign for delivery.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking one capacity figure as settled</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">TKO’s sell sheet says 350 lb and its manual says 150 kg. If you or your members are near the top of that range, get TKO’s answer for your unit in writing rather than relying on a dealer page.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not pricing freight and returns</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">This is a pallet delivery, kerbside with liftgate, and TKO charges a 15% restocking fee plus return shipping on change-of-mind returns. Get a delivered price, and decide whether you need inside delivery or installation, before comparing it with other machines.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for commercial features you will not use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Commercial build and app connectivity are the 8CTM’s strengths. If you train alone at home and do not use Zwift or Kinomap, a curved treadmill costing half as much may do everything you need.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

